  {
    "episode_id": 89163,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89163.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Hey, this is Trey. Thank you for joining us for another Tuesday's with Trey. We are grateful for your time and I am delighted to host someone that I served with in Congress. He is no stranger to you if you watch or follow the news. The gentleman from Ohio, Jim Jordan, but I can tell you what you see on air. is only part of the story. He works so hard, off air, preparing and reading and researching and checking with other members to make sure all the appropriate lines of inquiry are covered. He's got to take my word for it when I tell you, no one is going to outwork Jim Jordan and you may wind up liking what he comes up with and you may not like what he comes up with. But no one can question the work and the effort is there and the time has been spent. And the first thing you'll notice. when you work with Jim Jordan is that. And then very quickly after the work ethic, what I noticed was this devotion to his family. His wife, Polly, is quite candidly one of the kindest people I have ever met in my life. Their children were really, really, really good college athletes in different sports. Jim is modest. He's actually mile mannered off air, I would say. He doesn't raise. I've never heard him raise his voice in a conversation. He's always in pretty much the same mood, which is ready to work. So with that, I welcome the Congressman from the Gray State of Ohio, the ranking member of the Judiciary Committee and a part-time Intelligence Committee member, Jim Jordan. How are you doing, Jimmy?",
