  {
    "episode_id": 89163,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89163.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, the truth is I majored in wrestling, but you're supposed to get a degree. So the way it worked, at least when I was in school, I remember you had to, after your first semester, second year, sophomore year, you had to declare a major. I had no idea. I mean, like I said, I was majored in wrestling. But I took macroeconomics, I think it was macro 305. And there's, you know, typical lecture hall, University of Wisconsin, big university, 500 people in the lecture hall. Most of the athletes kind of sitting in the back and I was sitting in the back with some baseball players and some other people. The professor came out and he basically told us that he was kind of a Christian guy and a conservative kind of guy. And I'm like, holy cow, this is Madison, Wisconsin. This guy's either the craziest man on campus, or he's got tenure, or probably both. But I actually paid attention. And I kind of liked the class. And I thought, well, what the heck? I'll just major in economics. I got nothing else to major in, other than, as I said, the sport of wrestling. And so literally, I declared my major into that. I'm an economics major, wound up majoring in economics and then went into coaching after that a little bit. And while I was coaching, I went to grad school and then finally went to law school.",
