  {
    "episode_id": 89163,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89163.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "Well, you're thinking about going back home there once while just simply because we got eight acres, I get to mow four with my zero turn mower and I got my chainsaw and we'd eat and all that stuff I love to do. We got the creek right there, you remember in our front yard, we now have a bridge over. When you were there, we didn't have the bridge, we got this bridge over. So yeah, I love all that, but I also like, I like Congress and the idea that you're here to fight for the families back home and to truly try. I mean, it sounds cliche, but to truly try to do what you told them you were going to do. And the part I like is, and I know it's the part you liked about Congress as well, is when there's a person on the witness stand who I think hasn't been square with the American people, My job on behalf of the folks back in the 4th District of Ohio is to try to point out the truth and get the truth out there for the country to see. And you did it an amazing way. I will still remember your first term, maybe second, got to remember the year, but I will still remember the moment. Darryl Eis is chairman and Lois Lerner is testifying and she won't answer the questions. And you said, answer our questions. We deserve an answer. She was able to say a couple of things gave a statement. And it was such a powerful moment. And literally in so many ways, Trey, what you did was I think so much of the country then started to understand the abuses that were happening in certain agencies in our government. And it started with that moment when you called her out. And if you remember, we were starting to get to the bottom of, oh, this was targeting by the IRS, an agency that has a lot of influence over Americans' lives for goodness sake. And if they're coming after people for their political beliefs, their first amendment right to speak in a political fashion and not be harassed for doing so, That was huge. But that moment was sort of the catalyst for like, this is serious, this is real. And now we've seen it progress to what the FBI did to President Trump, to what the Justice Department's doing to moms and dads, to this, to you name it, it's the scary stuff that's going on. And so it's important work, I think. And I like, just like you, I like trying to get to the bottom of what's really going on here for the folks back home.",
