    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89163.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "It's the worst. There's so many days. I mean, you know the same frustration, but it's funny. My routine is I bet kind of close to yours because I used to watch you do this as well, but I'm old fashioned, maybe I'm just, maybe I'm just old. But to me, it's the yellow legal pad. And if you got a big hearing and important witness, I start with, I just start writing down questions. Like if I had unlimited time, I would ask, you know, I'd have 20 questions. And then of course you got to hone it down. Sometimes you sometimes in five minutes, you can really only ask one question. but you can ask it 10 times, right? You know what I mean? Other time you know this, sometimes you just want a statement and you want to lead in, you know how this works. And so, and then I'll get an idea of where I kind of want to go and then you got a good staff and you sit around with them and you figure out, okay, do I want the punch to the face question? Do I want a little narrative and story to front in and then the question? And it all just depends on the circumstances and it depends on the witness. Um, but that's how we go about it. But yeah, it's five minutes goes way too particularly when particularly when you got them on the run and you can feel it. I mean, I still remember one time with former attorney general Loretta Lynch, man, I was going the way I wanted it and it's like. I'd love another three or four minutes in that one. But you know the feeling.",
